# Alerting-pipeline watch — push to ntfy `yolo-private-cloud-resource-crunch` when the
# ALERTING PIPELINE itself is broken (Prometheus down, Alertmanager down, Watchdog alert
# missing, ntfy webhook failing). Was the resource watcher until 2026-08-04; the resource
# thresholds it owned moved into Alertmanager, which now notifies ntfy directly. This runs
# OUTSIDE the cluster on purpose — it is what still speaks when the cluster is the thing
# that broke, which Alertmanager structurally cannot do for itself.
# Every 5 min; the script debounces (3 consecutive samples = 15 sustained minutes before
# it speaks, then at most hourly, plus one "cleared" note), so this cadence is sampling
# frequency, NOT notification frequency. Cheap: two curls + two instant queries.
*/5 * * * * /home/cloud/Ideaprojects/STEP0/alerting-pipeline-watch.sh >> /home/cloud/Ideaprojects/STEP0/logs/alerting-pipeline-watch.cron.log 2>&1

# Docker build-cache cap (node AND host) + orphaned build-cache volume reclaim — bound
# /var against Jenkins build churn (docs/plan.md R7). EVERY 6 HOURS, was daily 04:30.
# Why the change (2026-08-04): a cap bounds the FLOOR after a prune, not the PEAK.
# Peak = cap + what accumulates between runs, and that accumulation is ~10GB/day of
# cold buildkit tail — which is how /var hit 90% and the kubelet declared
# DiskPressure=True (tainting the node, leaving Prometheus itself Pending) while this
# job had been running daily and its cap was correctly sized the whole time.
# 00:30 still lands before the Monday 05:00 root backup.
CRON_TZ=Europe/London
30 */6 * * * /home/cloud/Ideaprojects/STEP0/reduce-node-docker-cache.sh >> /home/cloud/Ideaprojects/STEP0/logs/reduce-node-docker-cache.log 2>&1

# Registry blob prune — weekly, Sunday 04:00. Until 2026-08-07 prune-registry.sh existed
# but was scheduled NOWHERE, so nothing ever reclaimed per-build image tags: by 2026-08-06
# that was 8 services x 302 `bNNNN` tags and a 37GB blob store. It was unschedulable
# because a garbage-collect racing an in-flight push can corrupt the store, and "run with
# Jenkins idle" was enforced only by printing a reminder to a human.
# The script now CHECKS the Jenkins queue itself and exits 0 without touching anything if a
# build is running, queued, or Jenkins is unreachable — so a missed week is a non-event and
# this is safe to automate. Sunday 04:00 is simply the quietest slot; the guard is what
# makes it correct, not the timing.
0 4 * * 0 /home/cloud/Ideaprojects/STEP0/prune-registry.sh --apply >> /home/cloud/Ideaprojects/STEP0/logs/prune-registry.log 2>&1
